<article class="env-lemma" id="0CZF">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CZF">Lemma <span data-tag="0CZF">75.7.4</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Consider a cartesian diagram of algebraic spaces over $S$ </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ X' \ar[d]_{f'} \ar[r]_{g'} &  X \ar[d]^ f \\  Y' \ar[r]^ g &  Y }  \]
</div>
  <p> with $f$ locally of finite type. If $T$ is a closed subset of $|X|$ proper over $Y$, then $|g'|^{-1}(T)$ is a closed subset of $|X'|$ proper over $Y'$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Observe that the statement makes sense as $f'$ is locally of finite type by Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/03XH" data-tag="03XH">67.23.3</a>. Let $Z \subset X$ be the reduced induced closed subspace structure on $T$. Denote $Z' = (g')^{-1}(Z)$ the scheme theoretic inverse image. Then $Z' = X' \times _ X Z = (Y' \times _ Y X) \times _ X Z = Y' \times _ Y Z$ is proper over $Y'$ as a base change of $Z$ over $Y$ (Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/04WP" data-tag="04WP">67.40.3</a>). On the other hand, we have $T' = |Z'|$. Hence the lemma holds. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>